"Governor Newsom issues executive order to accelerate independent oversight and advance the creation of an AI kill switch | Governor of California (https://www.gov.ca.gov/2026/09/18/governor-newsom-issues-executive-order-to-accelerate-independent-oversight-and-advance-the-creation-of-an-ai-kill-switch/)\nciteturn933view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn932view0\",\"id\":46}); Total lines: 129\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHouse passes bill aimed at impact of data centers on energy costs | AP News (https://apnews.com/article/ai-data-centers-congress-f073380caa61b720fa424590b5bc7c87)\nciteturn933view1 [wordlim: 100] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn932view3\",\"pattern\":\"WASHINGTON\"}); Total lines: 1923\nL1775: # House passes bill aimed at addressing impact of data centers on energy costs\nL1776: \nL1777: cite177†Image: Meta data centers operate, Saturday, Sept. 5, 2026, near Social Circle, Ga. (AP Photo/Mike Stewart)†dims.apnews.com L1778: \nL1779: Meta data centers operate, Saturday, Sept. 5, 2026, near Social Circle, Ga. (AP Photo/Mike Stewart)\nL1780: \nL1781: cite178†License this photo†newsroom.ap.org L1782: \nL1783: By  cite179†KEVIN FREKING L1784: \nL1785: \nL1786: \nL1787: Add AP News on Google Add AP News as your preferred source to see more of our stories on Google. Share\nL1788: cite180†Image: Comments†dims.apnews.com L1789: \nL1790: Share\nL1791: \nL1792:   * cite181†Facebook†www.facebook.com L1793:   * Copy\nL1794: \nL1795: Link copied\nL1796: \nL1797:   * Print\nL1798:   * cite182†Email L1799:   * cite183†X†twitter.com L1800:   * cite184†LinkedIn L1801:   * cite185†Bluesky†bsky.app L1802:   * cite186†Flipboard†share.flipboard.com L1803:   * cite187†Pinterest†pinterest.com L1804:   * cite188†Reddit†www.reddit.com L1805: WASHINGTON (AP) — The House overwhelmingly approved a bill Wednesday that aims to have artificial intelligence data centers pay for their substantial energy needs as lawmakers used what was expected to be their final day in Washington before the midterm elections to address a cite189†growing voter concern. L1806: The legislation would require state utility regulators to consider a standard by which utilities charge data centers for the full cost of the new power and transmission upgrades needed to serve them. The bill passed by a vote of 417-3.\nL1807: \nL1808: Supporters of the bill described the legislation as a “modest first start” in protecting families and businesses from higher utility bills. It essentially provides a federal recommendation while preserving state power to regulate electricity markets.\nL1809: “I promise that this bill is not the end of our focus on these issues. It’s only the beginning,” said Rep. Frank Pallone, the ranking Democrat on the House Energy and Commerce Committee.\nL1810: Some lawmakers are calling for a cite190†full moratorium on AI data centers until safeguards are put in place that ensure AI is safe and effective and that the data centers don’t increase utility prices or harm the environment. Meanwhile, more than 100 Democrats signed onto a letter this week that called on House Speaker Mike Johnson to keep the House in session this month until Congress advances meaningful artificial intelligence safeguards.\nL1811: Johnson said the bill that passed the House “strikes the right balance of protecting local communities while keeping America on the cutting edge of AI development.”\nL1812: \nL1813: The debate over the bill offered different views on the merits of data centers. House Majority Leader Steve Scalise, R-La., said the development of a data center in Richland Parish in his home state generated bonuses of up to $50,000 for local teachers as a result of increased tax collections.\nL1814: \nL1815: Read More\nL1816: “Those are the stories you’re seeing in communities that have embraced it,” Scalise said.\nL1817: \nL1818: Sign up for the Ground Game Newsletter: Your guide to the biggest stories in politics, policy and U.S. elections.\nL1819: \nL1820: [Input]\nL1821: \nL1822: Email address [Input: Email address]\nL1823: \nL1824: [Button: Sign up ]\nL1825: \nL1826: [Input] By checking this box, you agree to AP's cite164†Terms of Use and acknowledge that AP may collect and use your data pursuant to our cite165†Privacy Policy .\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSpain's data agency gets first report of AI-powered data breach (https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/spains-data-agency-gets-first-report-of-ai-powered-data-breach/)\nciteturn933view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn932view4\",\"pattern\":\"AEPD\"}); Total lines: 114\nL0: [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input: Search Site]\nL1: \nL2: [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: # Spain's data agency gets first report of AI-powered data breach\nL5: \nL6: By\nL7: ###### cite0†Bill Toulas L8: \nL9:   * September 16, 2026\nL10:   * 01:26 PM\nL11:   * cite1†0 L12: \nL13: cite2†Image: Spain reports first alleged AI-powered data theft attack†www.bleepstatic.com L14: \nL15: The Spanish Data Protection Agency (AEPD) was notified of an attack allegedly carried out with an AI agent powered by a known large language model (LLM).\nL16: The organization reporting the incident said that the AI agent searched for flaws, logged into their systems, and then probed apps for additional security issues. In the final stages of the attack, the agent modified personal data and accessed financial documents.\nL17: \nL18: Although the Spanish agency has yet to investigate the incident and verify the information, the AEPD says the notification shows AI-related data breaches are no longer merely theoretical.\nL19: “The attacking agent began searching for vulnerabilities in generic files and successfully logged in,” cite3†describes AEPD.†www.aepd.es L20: \nL21: “Once it gained access to the system, it began autonomously searching for vulnerabilities in the application. After finding them, it was able to modify personal data and access invoices.”\nL22: AEPD underlined that AI does not create new threats, but it can increase the speed, scale, and adaptability of cyberattacks, as well as reduce defenders' response-time margins, a paradigm shift recently highlighted by the country's cite4†National Cryptologic Center†www.ccn.cni.es .\nL23: \nL24: The notification signals a shift in risk management, which should explicitly account for AI-assisted and AI-driven attacks, as automation can affect an incident’s likelihood, speed, and scope.\nL25: Response time procedures should also be revised, since actions designed for manual attacks may be insufficient against agents that simultaneously analyze assets, test access methods, and adapt their behavior.\nL26: \nL27: AEPD also highlights the importance of strengthening digital identity and credential security, because agents can use compromised accounts, API keys, or tokens with excessive permissions to access multiple services at machine speed.\nL28: Manual intervention is no longer sufficient, and human oversight should be supported by fast detection, containment, and response mechanisms.\nL29: \nL30: \"The arrival of AI agents in the offensive arena should prompt an immediate review of security and data protection models,\" the Spanish agency warns.\nL31: Even if the AEPD confirms that autonomous AI was used in the reported data breach, the agency says this would not necessarily mean that the model powering the attack or its provider’s infrastructure was compromised, or that the model was designed to facilitate malicious cyber operations.\nL32: \nL33: Agentic attack activity has been reported recently in large-scale cyber operations. OpenAI’s agents escaped a testing environment and coordinated an cite5†intrusion into Hugging Face ’s production infrastructure.\nL34: Threat actors used Google Gemini multi-agent systems to scan for vulnerabilities and cite6†mass credential theft , and Anthropic Claude to cite7†scan 1.8 million Android apps for secrets left in the code.\nL35: ## cite8†Build your security blueprint for AI-powered attacks†hubs.li L36: \nL37: Join Mikko Hyppönen and security leaders from the NFL, CHANEL, and Atlassian for a two-hour digital summit on what AI-speed attacks change, what defenders should stop doing, and how to validate, decide, fix, and re-validate at machine speed.\nL38: \nL39: cite8†Save your seat†hubs.li L40: \nL41:   * cite9†Agentic AI L42:   * cite10†AI L43:   * cite11†AI Agent L44:   * cite12†Artificial Intelligence L45:   * cite13†Data Protection L46:   * cite14†Data Theft L47:   * cite15†Spain L48:   *   *   *   *   * ##### cite0†Bill Toulas bill.toulas@bleepingcomputer.com\nL49: \nL50: Bill Toulas is a tech writer and infosec news reporter with over a decade of experience working on various online publications, covering open-source, Linux, malware, data breach incidents, and hacks.\nL51: \nL52:   * cite16†Previous Article L53:   * cite17†Next Article L54: ##### Post a Comment cite18†Community Rules L55: ###### You need to login in order to post a comment\nL56: \nL57: [Input]\nL58: \nL59: Not a member yet? cite19†Register Now L60: \nL61:   Upcoming Webinar\nL62: \nL63: Popular Stories\nL64: \nL65:   * cite20†Cisco warns of max severity ISE zero-day exploited in attacks L66:   * cite21†Microsoft shares workaround for Windows domain login issues L67:   * cite22†Brevo supply-chain attack injected ClickFix scripts on customer sites L68: \nL69: S ponsor Posts\nL70:   * cite23†Patch automation needs more than speed. Action1 brings control into every stage of deployment.†www.action1.com L71:   * cite24†Overdue a password health-check? Audit your Active Directory for free†specopssoft.co